### What is the core problem?

The service receives 50,000 requests per second during a spike, but the SMS provider can only handle 10,000 messages per minute. You cannot forward every request; you must reduce the number of provider calls and pace the rest.

```java
package com.bank.otp.model;

import java.time.Instant;

public class OtpRecord {
    private String userId;       // e.g. "CUST-RAJESH-001"
    private String otpCode;      // e.g. "482913"
    private String phoneNumber;
    private Instant createdAt;
    private Instant expiresAt;
    private int sendCount;       // how many times this OTP was sent

    public OtpRecord(String userId, String otpCode, String phoneNumber,
                     int ttlSeconds) {
        this.userId = userId;
        this.otpCode = otpCode;
        this.phoneNumber = phoneNumber;
        this.createdAt = Instant.now();
        this.expiresAt = createdAt.plusSeconds(ttlSeconds);
        this.sendCount = 1;
    }

    public boolean isValid() {
        return Instant.now().isBefore(expiresAt);
    }

    public String getOtpCode() { return otpCode; }
    public void incrementSendCount() { sendCount++; }
}
```

### How does the Resend fix work?

When a user taps Resend, check if a valid OTP already exists. If yes, re-send it. If no, generate a new one. This collapses 5 taps into 1 generation plus 4 cheap re-sends.

```java
package com.bank.otp.service;

import com.bank.otp.model.OtpRecord;
import java.security.SecureRandom;

public class OtpService {

    private final OtpStore otpStore; // Redis-backed
    private final SmsGateway smsGateway;
    private final SecureRandom random = new SecureRandom();

    public OtpService(OtpStore otpStore, SmsGateway smsGateway) {
        this.otpStore = otpStore;
        this.smsGateway = smsGateway;
    }

    public String requestOtp(String userId, String phoneNumber) {
        // Check for existing valid OTP
        OtpRecord existing = otpStore.getActiveOtp(userId);
        if (existing != null && existing.isValid()) {
            // Resend the SAME OTP, do NOT generate a new one
            existing.incrementSendCount();
            smsGateway.send(phoneNumber, "Your OTP is: " + existing.getOtpCode());
            return existing.getOtpCode();
        }

        // Generate new OTP
        String code = String.format("%06d", random.nextInt(1_000_000));
        OtpRecord record = new OtpRecord(userId, code, phoneNumber, 300);
        otpStore.save(userId, record); // Redis SET with 300s TTL
        smsGateway.send(phoneNumber, "Your OTP is: " + code);
        return code;
    }
}
```

```text
# Rajesh taps "Send OTP" -> generates 482913, sends SMS (1 provider call)
# Rajesh taps "Resend" -> existing 482913 is valid, re-sends (1 cheap call)
# Rajesh taps "Resend" again -> same 482913 (1 cheap call)
# Without the fix: 3 taps = 3 new OTPs = 3 full provider calls
# With the fix: 3 taps = 1 new OTP + 2 re-sends (rate-limited)
```

### How does multi-level rate limiting work?

Each level catches a different abuse pattern. The per-provider token bucket is what makes failover work.

```java
package com.bank.otp.ratelimit;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class MultiLevelRateLimiter {

    // Per-user: max 3 OTPs in 5 minutes
    public boolean checkUserLimit(String userId) {
        int count = getUserOtpCount(userId);
        return count < 3;
    }

    // Per-IP: max 10 OTPs in 1 minute (escalate to CAPTCHA)
    public boolean checkIpLimit(String ipAddress) {
        int count = getIpOtpCount(ipAddress);
        return count < 10;
    }

    // Per-provider: token bucket at contractual rate
    public boolean checkProviderLimit(String providerId) {
        return getProviderBucket(providerId).tryConsume();
    }

    // Global: max 100,000 OTPs system-wide per minute
    public boolean checkGlobalLimit() {
        return getGlobalBucket().tryConsume();
    }

    // Stubs for brevity
    private int getUserOtpCount(String u) { return 0; }
    private int getIpOtpCount(String i) { return 0; }
    private TokenBucket getProviderBucket(String p) { return new TokenBucket(); }
    private TokenBucket getGlobalBucket() { return new TokenBucket(); }
}
```

### How does the TTL-and-priority queue absorb the burst?

Instead of calling the provider synchronously, enqueue send jobs. Workers drain at the provider's rate. Expired jobs are discarded.

```java
package com.bank.otp.queue;

import java.time.Instant;

public class OtpSendJob {
    private String phoneNumber;
    private String message;
    private int priority;       // 1=high-value transfer, 2=login
    private Instant expiresAt;  // discard if past this time

    public boolean isExpired() {
        return Instant.now().isAfter(expiresAt);
    }

    public int getPriority() { return priority; }
}

