### How does provider failover work?

Multiple providers, each with its own token bucket. When one is exhausted or unhealthy, route to the next.

```java
package com.bank.otp.provider;

import java.util.List;

public class FailoverSmsGateway {

    private final List<SmsProvider> providers; // ordered: primary, secondary

    public FailoverSmsGateway(List<SmsProvider> providers) {
        this.providers = providers;
    }

    public boolean send(String phoneNumber, String message) {
        for (SmsProvider provider : providers) {
            if (provider.isHealthy() && provider.hasCapacity()) {
                boolean sent = provider.send(phoneNumber, message);
                if (sent) return true;
            }
        }
        return false; // all providers exhausted
    }
}
```

### What about TOTP as a provider-independent fallback?

A time-based OTP is generated on the user's device from a shared secret and a clock. It needs no SMS, no network call, and zero provider load.

```java
package com.bank.otp.totp;

import javax.crypto.Mac;
import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

public class TotpGenerator {

    private static final int PERIOD = 30; // seconds
    private static final int DIGITS = 6;

    public static String generateTotp(byte[] secret) throws Exception {
        long timeStep = System.currentTimeMillis() / 1000 / PERIOD;
        byte[] timeBytes = ByteBuffer.allocate(8).putLong(timeStep).array();

        Mac mac = Mac.getInstance("HmacSHA1");
        mac.init(new SecretKeySpec(secret, "HmacSHA1"));
        byte[] hash = mac.doFinal(timeBytes);

        int offset = hash[hash.length - 1] & 0x0F;
        int code = ((hash[offset] & 0x7F) << 24
                  | (hash[offset + 1] & 0xFF) << 16
                  | (hash[offset + 2] & 0xFF) << 8
                  | (hash[offset + 3] & 0xFF)) % (int) Math.pow(10, DIGITS);

        return String.format("%06d", code);
    }
}
```

```text
# TOTP for Rajesh: 738291 (valid for 30 seconds)
# No SMS needed. No provider call. Zero load on the spike.
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

How would you handle the case where the user's TOTP secret is compromised? How do you prevent OTP brute-force attacks? What is the maximum number of verification attempts you allow? How do you handle users who cannot receive SMS in certain countries? How does the Resend cooldown interact with the per-user rate limit?

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer measures the actual retry-storm multiplier before and after the Resend fix, monitors per-provider token-bucket fill rates, and sets up alerts for when the primary provider nears capacity so failover activates before total exhaustion. They also audit OTP brute-force attempts with a maximum verification-attempt limit per OTP.

```java
package com.bank.otp;

public class SeniorOtpGuidelines {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior OTP guidelines:");
        System.out.println("  1. Measure retry-storm multiplier: N taps / N provider calls");
        System.out.println("  2. Max verification attempts per OTP: 3 (block after 3 wrong)");
        System.out.println("  3. Alert when primary provider bucket < 20% capacity");
        System.out.println("  4. Audit log every OTP request: userId, IP, provider, timestamp");
        System.out.println("  5. TOTP enrollment reduces provider dependency permanently");
        System.out.println("  6. Never log or cache the OTP code in plain text");
        System.out.println("  7. Use SecureRandom, not Random, for OTP generation");
    }
}
```

